1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Sixteen sorted cells, one target. Linear search would touch up to sixteen of them.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Probe the middle. One comparison tells you which half the target must be in.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Eight cells left. Halve again: 47 is too big, so the answer is in 35 to 43.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Four probes on 16 cells. Doubling the array costs one more probe, never twice as many.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Three indices, one sentence: if the target is in the array at all, it sits inside [lo, hi].

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Equal: stop. Smaller than the middle: the answer is left. Larger: the answer is right.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The middle cell is excluded from both new ranges, because it has just been tested.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
When lo passes hi the range is empty, the invariant is still true, and the answer is "absent".

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Trace one: find 27 in sixteen cells. The table records lo, hi, mid and the verdict of each probe.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Probe 31, then 14, then 23, then 27: four comparisons, and the fourth is the hit.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Trace two: find 25, which is not there. The loop empties the range in four probes.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Both runs used the same number of probes. Success and failure are not different costs here.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
One comparison, then the same problem on half the cells: F(n) = F(floor(n/2)) + 1.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Unrolling gives floor(log2 n) + 1, and the table confirms it for n = 1 to 16.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
floor(log2 n) + 1 and ceil(log2(n+1)) agree for every n, so both answer forms are safe.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Best case is one probe. Average depends on which targets you assume, so state the assumption.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Shape one: lo <= hi, hi = mid - 1. Shape two: lo < hi, hi = mid. Both are correct.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Bug one: lo = mid inside lo < hi. When hi = lo + 1 the middle is lo, and nothing moves.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Bug two: (lo + hi) / 2 overflows. With 32-bit ints the sum turns negative above 2^31 - 1.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Mid by floor or by ceiling: with lo < hi and hi = mid, use the floor. The ceiling can stall.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
With repeats, "found" is ambiguous. Three different questions hide behind one array.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
lower_bound: the first index whose value is not less than the target. The array need not contain it.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
upper_bound: the first index whose value is greater. Its difference from lower_bound counts the copies.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
One loop can do both: shrink on the right when the value is big enough, on the left when it is small enough.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A rotated sorted array is two sorted runs. 43, 47, 51, 3, 7, 11, 14 is one example.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Compare the left end with the middle. If the left end is smaller, the left half is the sorted one.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
If the target lies inside the sorted run, go there. Otherwise go to the other half.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The bound is unchanged: one comparison plus one half. Rotation costs nothing asymptotically.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A bitonic array rises to one peak and then falls. It is neither sorted nor rotated.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Find the peak by comparing the middle with its right neighbour: bigger means go right.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Then search the ascending run normally, and the descending run with the comparison reversed.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Three logarithmic stages added, not multiplied: Theta(log n). The official key is log n.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Sometimes nothing is sorted. What you have instead is a yes/no question that flips once.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Search the range of possible answers, not the data. Same loop, different meaning of the middle.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Write the predicate, prove it monotone, then the loop is the same ten lines as before.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The cost is the log of the range, not of the data: log of the largest possible answer.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Any comparison search is a tree. Each probe is a node, each outcome an edge, each answer a leaf.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
A tree of height h has at most 2^h - 1 internal nodes, so n <= 2^h - 1 and h >= ceil(log2(n+1)).

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Ternary search pays two comparisons per level to cut to a third. It is slower, not faster.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Five questions settle any item: sorted? which count? which shape? present or absent? which assumption?

